\section{Value rules}{

Both helpers divide within each fixed \code{.by} partition, so a fixed key never
contributes to another partition's denominator, and neither ever
synthesizes or completes keys. A missing numerator, missing denominator, or
zero denominator gives \code{NA_real_}; local \code{NaN} is missing. Other finite
ratios are unclamped doubles, so negative values and values above one are
retained. Denominator matching uses internal grouping set metadata rather
than \code{.id} or displayed Margin labels, and missing values of fixed keys and
Margin dimensions are matched with missing-safe identity rather than
ordinary SQL \code{NULL = NULL}.

The portable guarantee covers finite numbers, missing values, and zero
denominators. Infinite values and backend-specific \code{NaN} representations are
outside it, because supported SQL dialects do not share one portable
finite-value predicate; normalize such summaries explicitly with operations
the backend supports.

For a Parent share, composite dimensions are added or removed together.
The Grand total set has no parent, so its Parent share is \code{1.0}, even when
its source is zero or missing. Duplicate grouping set occurrences remain in
the result but are skipped while finding the parent, because a parent must
be strictly less detailed. For \code{rollup(region, store)}, a store's parent is
its region subtotal and a region subtotal's parent is the grand total. In
the first example below, Boston in January 2025 has \code{3600 / 7500 = 0.48}:
its revenue over the East subtotal for that month.

Every row of the Grand total set, duplicate occurrences included, has a
Total share of \code{1.0}, even when its source is zero or missing. Which grand
total occurrence supplies the denominator is not specified.

Empty input gives one Grand total set row or no rows at all, and the share
column keeps its type either way:\tabular{lrl}{
   Empty input \tab Rows \tab Share value and type \cr
   Without fixed \code{.by} keys \tab One row of the Grand total set \tab \code{1.0}, double \cr
   With fixed \code{.by} keys \tab Zero rows \tab Empty double vector \cr
}


Missing detail or subtotal combinations are not completed. \href{https://sayuks.github.io/marginplyr/vignettes/completing_keys.html}{Complete absent keys before margins} shows how to complete the input facts before the
summary, where synthetic facts are meant to reach every level.
}

\section{Direct shares}{

A direct call must be the complete right-hand side of an explicitly named
summary, and its argument the bare name of one eligible ordinary summary
written earlier in the same call:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{# Supported
total_revenue = sum(revenue)
revenue_share = share_of_parent(total_revenue)

# Rejected: aggregate or calculated arguments; rewrite as above
revenue_share = share_of_parent(sum(revenue))
revenue_share = share_of_parent(revenue + tax)

# Rejected: wrapping or scaling the helper, or a later summary using it
revenue_percent = 100 * share_of_parent(total_revenue)
revenue_percent = 100 * revenue_share
# Rewrite: derive from the finished share afterwards
result |> dplyr::mutate(revenue_percent = 100 * revenue_share)
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

On a lazy input that \code{mutate()} wraps the result in a subquery, which drops
an order \code{.sort} requested; \code{\link[dplyr:collect]{dplyr::collect()}} first, or \code{\link[dplyr:arrange]{dplyr::arrange()}}
afterwards, as \emph{Execute in DuckDB} in \href{https://sayuks.github.io/marginplyr/vignettes/database_backends.html#execute-in-duckdb}{Database and lazy backends}
shows.
}

\section{Eligible source summaries}{

A source summary must be:
\itemize{
\item defined exactly once, as a top-level named summary or a statically named
output of a preceding \code{\link[dplyr:across]{dplyr::across()}};
\item self-contained: it may aggregate input columns, but it cannot refer to an
earlier summary, which databases that cannot reuse a \code{SELECT} alias could
not evaluate;
\item one plain integer or double per grouping row, or the database's
numeric or decimal equivalent.
}

An \code{across()} output is statically named when its name is known before the
call runs. An \code{across()} written after another summary is evaluated only
once that summary has run, so it qualifies only when what it reads already
holds a value; an argument of your own function holds none until it is
used. Evaluate the argument first with \code{\link[=force]{force()}}, or write the \code{across()}
before the other summaries.

Logical, complex, date, date-time, duration, character, factor, list,
data-frame, and other classes are not converted implicitly. Convert
intentionally in the ordinary summary:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{# Rejected: a Date summary is not a numeric measure
first_date = min(date)
date_share = share_of_parent(first_date)

# Supported when the conversion has domain meaning
first_day = as.double(min(date))
day_share = share_of_parent(first_day)
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

A zero-length or multi-value summary cannot be matched to one denominator
row. A quantile call with several probabilities is one common multi-value
result; define each statistic as a separate summary:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{# Rejected
revenue_quantile = stats::quantile(revenue, c(0.25, 0.75))
quantile_share = share_of_parent(revenue_quantile)

# Supported
revenue_q25 = stats::quantile(revenue, 0.25)
revenue_q75 = stats::quantile(revenue, 0.75)
q25_share = share_of_parent(revenue_q25)
q75_share = share_of_parent(revenue_q75)
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

A redefined source and one built from an earlier summary have the same
rewrite, one complete calculation:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{# Rejected: `net` is defined twice
net = sum(revenue)
net = net - sum(discount)

# Rejected: `net` refers to the earlier summary `gross`
gross = sum(revenue)
net = gross - sum(discount)

# Supported
net = sum(revenue) - sum(discount)
net_share = share_of_parent(net)
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

A column expanded from an unnamed data-frame-valued summary is not
top-level named: dplyr expands its columns into the result, but their
provenance is not static enough for a share's dependency:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{# Rejected
tibble::tibble(revenue_total = sum(revenue))
revenue_share = share_of_parent(revenue_total)

# Supported
revenue_total = sum(revenue)
revenue_share = share_of_parent(revenue_total)
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

Naming an \code{\link[dplyr:across]{dplyr::across()}} does the mirror image of that:
\code{total = across(c(revenue, units), sum)} packs both results into one
data-frame-valued \code{total} column, so \code{total} is not a scalar source. Drop
the name to get one statically named column per selected column, or define
each summary at top level.

Final columns keep the written expression order, including the expansion
position of each \code{across()} call.
}

\section{Column-wise shares}{

In \code{across(.cols, share_of_parent, .names = ...)} and its
\code{share_of_total} counterpart, \code{.cols} sees only preceding eligible ordinary
summaries. Source-data columns, fixed \code{.by} keys, Margin
dimensions, and earlier shares are outside this selection context.
\code{\link[dplyr:everything]{dplyr::everything()}} therefore means every preceding ordinary summary, not
every input column.

Name-based tidyselect is supported: direct names, ranges, Boolean
combinations, positive and negative selections, \code{\link[dplyr:all_of]{dplyr::all_of()}},
\code{\link[dplyr:any_of]{dplyr::any_of()}}, \code{\link[dplyr:everything]{dplyr::everything()}}, and name-pattern helpers such as
\code{\link[dplyr:starts_with]{dplyr::starts_with()}}. Type/value predicates such as
\code{where(is.numeric)} are rejected because lazy backends do not reliably
expose arbitrary summary-result prototypes before execution. Select names
explicitly instead:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{# Rejected
across(where(is.numeric), share_of_parent, .names = "\{.col\}_share")

# Supported
across(c(revenue, units), share_of_parent, .names = "\{.col\}_share")
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

\code{.fns} must be one direct bare helper — \code{share_of_parent},
\code{share_of_total}, or either written as \code{marginplyr::share_of_parent}.
Formulas, anonymous functions, and function lists are rejected, even if
they contain only a helper. Use two ordered \code{across()} expressions so the
dependency is explicit:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{# Rejected
across(
  c(units, revenue),
  list(total = sum, share = share_of_parent)
)

# Supported
across(c(units, revenue), sum)
across(
  c(units, revenue),
  share_of_parent,
  .names = "\{.col\}_share"
)
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

Additional function arguments are rejected; missing-value handling belongs
to the preceding aggregate. \code{.unpack = TRUE} is also rejected because one
share is already one scalar double column:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{# Rejected
across(
  revenue,
  share_of_parent,
  na.rm = TRUE,
  .names = "\{.col\}_share",
  .unpack = TRUE
)

# Supported
revenue = sum(revenue, na.rm = TRUE)
across(
  revenue,
  share_of_parent,
  .names = "\{.col\}_share",
  .unpack = FALSE
)
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

\code{.names} is required. Generated names must be non-empty and unique and must
not collide with fixed keys, Margin dimensions, ordinary summaries,
source columns that remain in the result, \code{.id}, or another share of either
kind. Change the template or rename the conflicting output:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{# Rejected: overwrites each source summary
across(revenue, share_of_parent, .names = "\{.col\}")

# Supported
across(revenue, share_of_parent, .names = "\{.col\}_share")
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}
}

\section{Lazy execution boundaries}{

On a dbplyr input, what decides whether a share runs is how the SQL dialect
treats a source summary of the wrong type:
\itemize{
\item A dialect that refuses one itself, such as DuckDB, computes the share.
An ineligible source reaches you as the database's own error.
\item A dialect that converts one to a number instead, such as SQLite, and a
connection that executes nothing, such as a \verb{dbplyr::simulate_*()}
connection used for \code{\link[dplyr:show_query]{dplyr::show_query()}}, refuse the share by default.
\code{.check_share_source = FALSE} calculates it from sources you have
established yourself.
}

Syntax, source-name, written-order, and \code{across()} errors are reported
before execution on every backend, and no backend calculates a share from a
source it has shown to be ineligible. None of it reads a row of your data.
The summary judged is the one the backend computed, so an aggregate whose
result type differs across backends is eligible on some and refused on
others: \code{max()} over a logical column is an integer in R, which the rule
accepts, and a boolean on a database whose \code{MAX} returns one, which it
refuses.

What differs is what establishes the rule, and whether the
exactly-one-value cardinality rule is established with it:\tabular{ll}{
   Backend \tab What establishes the source rules \cr
   Local data frame \tab Both, from the result, before any share \cr
   \code{dtplyr} step \tab Both, at execution, before an invalid row \cr
   Arrow \tab Neither; shares are rejected outright \cr
   General dbplyr \tab Type from the dialect, and cardinality as below \cr
}


Arrow inputs refuse both helpers before any summary query is built; other
Arrow Margin operations stay lazy, as \emph{\link[=margin_verbs]{Arrow inputs}} in
\link{margin_verbs} says. Collect an Arrow input first to calculate shares
locally; \code{.check_margin_label} in \code{\link[=summarize_with_margins]{summarize_with_margins()}} says what
collecting changes. A \code{dtplyr} step
stays a lazy query, with nothing collected on your behalf, and its
execution-time diagnostics name the share, its source summary, and the
original call, as the local ones do.

On dbplyr, a summary that is not one value per grouping row surfaces as a
database error when the staged query executes. \emph{Parent shares use a staged
lazy query} in \href{https://sayuks.github.io/marginplyr/vignettes/database_backends.html#parent-shares-use-a-staged-lazy-query}{Database and lazy backends} describes how a dialect is
asked, which answers are reused, and what that sends; its \emph{Backend support
matrix} sets each backend family's share support beside its other answers.
}

\section{Forwarding a name from your own function}{

A function of your own forwards the name its caller wrote by embracing it,
as it would for dplyr: \code{share_of_parent({{ name }})}. The spellings \code{{{ }}}
abbreviates, \code{!!rlang::enquo(name)} and \code{!!rlang::ensym(name)}, are
accepted too, and a name held as a string is injected as
\code{!!rlang::sym(name)}. Only the name is read. It is resolved among the
preceding summaries, as every bare name here is, so the environment the
caller's expression came from is not consulted and an injection carrying
anything but a name is refused exactly where writing that expression out
would be.

\emph{Call a Margin verb from your own function} in
\href{https://sayuks.github.io/marginplyr/vignettes/recipes.html#call-a-margin-verb-from-your-own-function}{Recipes for common reporting tasks} names a summary after the
caller's measure and forwards that name here.
}

\examples{
# Both denominators in one call: the parent level, and the whole. Each
# helper names the summary `total_revenue`, not the input column `revenue`.
# Boston in January 2025 has a Parent share of 0.48 = 3600 / East 7500.
summarize_with_margins(
  .data = retail_sales,
  total_revenue = sum(revenue),
  revenue_share = share_of_parent(total_revenue),
  revenue_of_total = share_of_total(total_revenue),
  .by = c(year, month),
  .grouping = rollup(region, store)
)

# A Total share accepts any plan containing the Grand total set, so a cube
# can report what fraction of the whole each cell is.
summarize_with_margins(
  .data = retail_sales,
  revenue = sum(revenue),
  revenue_of_total = share_of_total(revenue),
  .by = year,
  .grouping = cube(region, store)
)

# Multiple measures use two ordered across() expressions. Selection for the
# second across() sees the ordinary summaries created by the first.
summarize_with_margins(
  .data = retail_sales,
  dplyr::across(c(units, revenue), sum),
  dplyr::across(
    c(units, revenue),
    share_of_total,
    .names = "{.col}_of_total"
  ),
  .by = c(year, month),
  .grouping = rollup(region, store)
)

# Derived percentages belong in a post-summary mutate().
parent_report <- summarize_with_margins(
  .data = retail_sales,
  revenue = sum(revenue),
  revenue_share = share_of_parent(revenue),
  .grouping = rollup(region, store)
)
dplyr::mutate(
  .data = parent_report,
  revenue_percent = 100 * revenue_share
)

# Missing fixed and included keys use missing-safe denominator lookup, and
# a missing fixed key is still its own partition. The Grouping bit
# distinguishes an included missing group from its subtotal.
missing_keys <- data.frame(
  partition = c(NA_character_, NA_character_, "B", "B"),
  group = c("x", NA_character_, "x", NA_character_),
  value = c(3, 1, 6, 2)
)
summarize_with_margins(
  .data = missing_keys,
  total = sum(value),
  share = share_of_parent(total),
  of_total = share_of_total(total),
  group_is_margin = grouping_bit(group),
  .by = partition,
  .grouping = rollup(group),
  .margin_label = NULL
)

# Empty input without `.by` has one Grand total set row, whose share is
# one. With `.by`, there are no partitions; both results retain a double
# share column.
empty_keys <- missing_keys[0, ]
empty_grand_total <- summarize_with_margins(
  .data = empty_keys,
  total = sum(value),
  share = share_of_parent(total),
  .grouping = rollup(group)
)
c(
  rows = nrow(empty_grand_total),
  type = typeof(empty_grand_total$share)
)
empty_partitions <- summarize_with_margins(
  .data = empty_keys,
  total = sum(value),
  share = share_of_parent(total),
  .by = partition,
  .grouping = rollup(group)
)
c(
  rows = nrow(empty_partitions),
  type = typeof(empty_partitions$share)
)

# Rejected forms report their supported context or rewrite, naming the
# helper that was written.
try(share_of_parent(revenue))
try(dplyr::summarize(
  .data = retail_sales,
  revenue_share = share_of_parent(revenue)
))
try(summarize_with_margins(
  .data = retail_sales,
  revenue = sum(revenue),
  dplyr::across(
    dplyr::where(is.numeric),
    share_of_total,
    .names = "{.col}_share"
  ),
  .grouping = rollup(region, store)
))

# A Parent share needs one pure rollup(); a Total share needs a plan that
# contains the Grand total set.
try(summarize_with_margins(
  .data = retail_sales,
  revenue = sum(revenue),
  revenue_share = share_of_parent(revenue),
  .grouping = cube(region, store)
))
try(summarize_with_margins(
  .data = retail_sales,
  revenue = sum(revenue),
  revenue_of_total = share_of_total(revenue),
  .grouping = grouping_sets(grouping_set(region), grouping_set(store))
))
}
